---
/**
 * The method behind the recommendations, in plain text. AI tools and search engines can't run
 * the calculator, but they can quote this. Numbers come from kitLogic where possible.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "../components/guide/Breadcrumb.astro";
import GuideCta from "../components/guide/GuideCta.astro";
import { getKitRecommendation } from "../lib/kitLogic";
import { INTENSITY_OPTIONS, WARMTH_LABELS } from "../components/calculator/constants";
import { toF } from "../lib/guideData";
import { abs, articleNode, breadcrumbNode } from "../lib/site";

const canonical = abs("/how-it-works/");
const title = "How kitcheck.cc decides what you should wear";
const description = "The method behind the kitcheck.cc cycling kit calculator: forecast data, effort, warmth tendency, wind, rain and why gloves and overshoes are chosen differently.";

const at10 = (o: Partial<Parameters<typeof getKitRecommendation>[0]>) =>
  getKitRecommendation({ apparentTemp: 10, windspeed: 10, precipProb: 0, warmthBias: 0, intensity: "moderate", ...o }).feelsLikeC;
const effort = INTENSITY_OPTIONS.map((o) => ({ label: o.label, desc: o.desc, c: at10({ intensity: o.value }) }));
const warmth = WARMTH_LABELS.map((label, i) => ({ label, c: at10({ warmthBias: i - 2 }) }));
const t = (c: number) => `${c}°C (${toF(c)}°F)`;
---

<Base
  title="How the Cycling Kit Calculator Works | kitcheck.cc"
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  schema={[
    articleNode({ url: canonical, headline: title, description, lang: "en" }),
    breadcrumbNode([{ name: "kitcheck.cc", url: abs("/") }, { name: "How it works", url: canonical }]),
  ]}
>
  <SiteHeader>
    <a href="/" class="btn-primary">Use the calculator</a>
  </SiteHeader>

  <section class="bg-bg">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="max-w-2xl pt-16 md:pt-18 lg:pt-20 pb-10 md:pb-12">
        <Breadcrumb items={[{ name: "kitcheck.cc", href: "/" }, { name: "How it works" }]} />
        <h1 class="font-head text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-[1.1] text-text mb-4">{title}</h1>
        <p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed">
          kitcheck.cc turns the weather forecast for your ride into a kit list for five body zones:
          head, upper body, hands, legs and feet. This page explains how, step by step, so you know
          what the recommendation is based on and where its limits are.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-bg border-t border-border">
    <div class="container py-12 md:py-16">
      <div class="max-w-2xl prose-custom">
        <h2>1. The forecast for your whole ride</h2>
        <p>
          The calculator takes the hourly forecast from <a href="https://open-meteo.com" rel="noopener">Open-Meteo</a> for
          your location and every hour of your ride window: the feels-like temperature (which already
          includes wind chill and humidity), wind speed and the chance of rain. It doesn't look at a
          single snapshot, so a cold start or rain later in the ride shows up in the advice.
        </p>
        <p>
          With a GPX route, it goes further: it takes the forecast at points along the route
          (about every 6 km, plus the highest point) for the time you'll pass them, at the right
          altitude, and judges the wind against your direction of travel. A headwind counts fully,
          a tailwind barely.
        </p>

        <h2>2. How it feels on the bike</h2>
        <p>
          Riding hard makes it feel much warmer than standing still; an easy spin feels colder. The
          calculator shifts the feels-like temperature for your effort level. With a forecast of {t(10)}:
        </p>
        <ul>
          {effort.map((e) => <li><strong>{e.label}</strong> ({e.desc.toLowerCase()}): feels like about {t(e.c)}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>
          How warm you naturally run matters too. Riders who are always cold get warmer kit, riders
          who always run warm get less. At a moderate pace and {t(10)}:
        </p>
        <ul>
          {warmth.map((w) => <li><strong>{w.label}</strong>: feels like about {t(w.c)}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>
          Strong wind adds a small extra penalty on top of the forecast's feels-like temperature,
          for the airflow of riding into it. From about 25 km/h (16 mph) a wind gilet is suggested
          as an option.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Hands, feet and head are different</h2>
        <p>
          Effort warms your body, but your fingers, toes and ears barely benefit. So the calculator
          counts effort only for about a third and warmth tendency for three quarters when it picks
          gloves, overshoes and head covering. That's why you can ride in a lighter jacket at race
          pace and still need winter gloves.
        </p>
        <p>
          Gloves and overshoes are also chosen for the whole ride: nobody carries a second pair. If
          it gets colder or starts raining later, you get the warmer pair from the start, with a
          note explaining why.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Rain and heat</h2>
        <p>
          From a 40% chance of rain, a packable rain jacket is added as an option. From 60%, rain is
          treated as likely and the rain kit scales with the temperature: a waterproof jacket,
          waterproof gloves and overshoes in the cold; a packable jacket and thin rain overshoes when
          it's mild; optional when it's warm. From a feels-like {t(27)}, a heat note reminds you to
          take an extra bottle and sunscreen.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Required and optional</h2>
        <p>
          Every zone gets required items and, where it's a judgement call, optional ones. Optional
          items sit in the band where riders genuinely disagree: arm warmers on a fresh spring
          morning, toe covers on a long descent. When conditions change during the ride, notes tell
          you when to take something off or put it on.
        </p>

        <h2>Limits</h2>
        <p>
          The recommendation is a well-informed starting point, not a guarantee. Forecasts can be
          wrong, especially for rain more than a day ahead, and people differ: use the warmth
          tendency setting if the advice is consistently too warm or too cold for you. The logic is
          built for road cycling; for mountain biking or commuting at low speed, you'll usually need
          a bit more.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/guide/">kit guide</a> and the <a href="/guide/cycling-temperature-chart/">temperature chart</a> are
          generated from the same logic as the calculator, so they never disagree with it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <GuideCta />
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
